From 79dfcc03fbd86bb2b0566c6cec520aac044148ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 18:54:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] replica run evidence: read the current run's newest
 liveness event outside the link-row memo

Review t_01a1d0f7c4cc4688 (B1): a pulled heartbeat task_event, plus the heartbeat
columns it bumps, is bookkeeping for kanban_activity_revision. The shadow content
key therefore never moved for it, so _read_replica served the memoised run_beat.
A live remote card froze on its first beat and expired to unknown 180 s later on
a quiet board, and a rolled-over run never got its first event.

- The run terms leave the memoised _read_replica_links. kanban_replica_execution.read_runs
  reads {task: run_uid, run_beat} on every _read_replica with ONE bound-parameter
  query, driven from this gateway's unreleased links (idx_task_chat_links_chat ->
  tasks PK -> task_runs PK -> task_events(run_id, id)). On a real default-board
  replica: ~4 ms incl. schema probe for 154 links / 3 running cards. The
  task-driven EXISTS form took 77 ms.
- The link-row memo, the shadow key, SHADOW_TABLES and the triggers are unchanged:
  a heartbeat still re-reads no board, links projection or counts by itself.
  The counts pick up the new beat on their next recompute, within their existing
  3 s TTL / 30 s revalidation bound, the same lag local heartbeats already have.
  The beat stays source-dated, so the lag only ever ages it. Never the pull clock.
- The SQL interpolates nothing; an assert pins the placeholder count to LIVENESS_KINDS.
- A trigger-backed kbc.connect shadow test covers memoised rows + new heartbeat,
  quiet-board run rollover (an old run's late beat is never served, and the new run's
  first event dates it), and sidebar counts + session_tasks seeing the same new beat.
  All 3 are RED at 18113d0e57 and GREEN here.
